\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figs/FigS1-Exemplars.pdf}
  \caption{Model~B regime exemplars from the Stage~2 factorial: a crash-prone run
  (run 9652) and an oscillatory run (run 5916).
  \textbf{(A)}~Price, its exponential moving average, and the EMA band for the
  crash-prone exemplar, a bubble-and-collapse trajectory ($100 \to 266 \to 83$).
  \textbf{(B)}~The same for the oscillatory exemplar, whose price stayed within a few
  percent of its starting value.
  \textbf{(C)}~Interior Buy, Hold, and Sell fractions for the crash-prone exemplar.
  \textbf{(D)}~The same for the oscillatory exemplar.
  \textbf{(E)}~Suppressed buy and sell orders per step for the crash-prone exemplar.
  \textbf{(F)}~The same for the oscillatory exemplar. The crash-prone exemplar is the
  extreme tail of its regime, shown because it makes the mechanism visible. The
  \emph{typical} crash-prone run is a liquidity freeze in which the grid locks near a
  Sell fraction of $0.88$ while its maximum drawdown stays near $5\%$. ``Crash-prone'' throughout
  denotes majority-sell lock-in (Sell fraction $\ge 0.5$ on at least 1\% of steps), not
  a price discontinuity.}
  \label{fig:s1}
\end{figure}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figs/FigS2-SeedTrajectories.pdf}
  \caption{Model~B seed sweep: largest-sell-cluster trajectories per seed, $20$ disorder
  seeds per budget configuration.
  \textbf{(A)}~Largest sell cluster, expressed as a fraction of the grid, drawn step by
  step for every seed of the stable configuration ($f = 0.10$, cash $1{,}000$).
  \textbf{(B)}~The same for the freeze configuration ($f = 0.20$, cash $500$).
  \textbf{(C)}~The same for the collapse configuration ($f = 0.20$, cash $5{,}000$). The
  time-shifted-sigmoid fan within each above-threshold configuration reflects stochastic
  nucleation waiting times rather than a spread of eventual outcomes.
  \textbf{(D)}~Per-seed ratio of the largest cluster to the total sell population at its
  peak. The stable configuration sits at a median ratio of $0.022$, dispersed
  fluctuations with no dominant domain, while the freeze and collapse configurations
  concentrate into a single growing domain at median ratios of $0.26$ and $0.44$, and
  the stable band shows zero overlap against either above-threshold configuration.
  \textbf{(E)}~The same comparison for the largest-cluster fraction of the grid at the
  end of the run.}
  \label{fig:s2}
\end{figure}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.55\linewidth]{figs/FigS3-PeakSell.pdf}
  \caption{Peak sell fraction against the minority fraction~$f$ for Model~A
  (Rules~19280/19286, $128 \times 128$ grid, fixed seed), the third summary of the sweep, alongside
  its mean sell fraction and crash frequency (Figure~\ref{fig:crossover}B
  and~\ref{fig:crossover}C). The dashed vertical line at $f = 0.145$ marks the midpoint of
  the observed $[0.14,0.15]$ crossover bracket, not a fitted thermodynamic critical
  point.}
  \label{fig:s3}
\end{figure}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figs/FigS4-BarrierParameters.pdf}
  \caption{Onset cliff and fitted barrier parameters across the cash ladder.
  \textbf{(A)}~Fraction of seeds that nucleated within the $2{,}000$-step horizon,
  against the minority fraction~$f$ at cash $500$, $1{,}000$, and $5{,}000$: the onset
  cliff sits between $f = 0.15$ and $f = 0.18$ at every cash level.
  \textbf{(B)}~Fitted pseudo-threshold $f_0$ per cash level with $95\%$ bootstrap
  confidence intervals; the dashed line marks the Model~A value $f_0 = 0.101$.
  \textbf{(C)}~Fitted shape coefficient $B$ on the same footing, against the Model~A
  value $B = 0.389$. The intervals overlap between tight and loose cash on both
  parameters. That overlap is not itself a test. The paired equivalence analysis
  reported in the text is the test, and it bounds the between-budget difference in predicted
  median onset time to within a factor of two. The resampling behind these intervals is
  described in Appendix~\ref{app:censoring}.
  \textbf{(D)}~Front velocity at $f = 0.20$ arranged by initial cash, one point per
  included run and a bar at each per-dose median: the medians are U-shaped in cash,
  highest at cash $250$, lowest near $1{,}000$--$2{,}000$, and rising again at
  $5{,}000$, while the per-dose median suppression spans only $517$--$595$ orders per
  step. The supported claim is therefore the pooled and within-dose
  velocity--suppression relationship (Figure~\ref{fig:growth-gating}C), not a monotone
  ordering by dose.}
  \label{fig:s4}
\end{figure}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figs/FigS5-SeedingDetail.pdf}
  \caption{Stamped-patch seeding detail across placements and configurations.
  \textbf{(A)}~Onset step per seed under each arm (an unstamped control and a
  $3 \times 3$ sell patch stamped at edge (boundary-adjacent), mid, and center
  placements) for the freeze
  configuration, with gray lines pairing the same seed across arms; the pairings cross in
  both directions and no placement advanced onset. The apparent delay under the freeze
  mid placement (median paired onset advance $-257$ steps, sign-flip $p = 0.0238$) is
  exploratory, uncorrected across the six-test family and failing the Bonferroni
  threshold of $0.0083$, a lead rather than a result.
  \textbf{(B)}~The same for the collapse configuration.
  \textbf{(C)}~Dominance rate per placement for the freeze configuration: the fraction of
  runs in which the cluster standing largest at onset descends from the stamped patch is
  $0/20$ in each of the three stamped placements, so the bars are drawn at zero height
  and the panel states the count as text.
  \textbf{(D)}~The same for the collapse configuration, also $0/20$ in each stamped placement, which
  completes the six configuration-by-placement cells of the design.
  \textbf{(E)}~Largest-cluster growth trajectories for a single exemplar seed under each
  arm in the freeze configuration.
  \textbf{(F)}~The same in the collapse configuration. Which arm reaches domain scale
  first differs between the two configurations rather than tracking the stamped
  placement, and the domain that grows is in every run one founded away from the patch.}
  \label{fig:s5}
\end{figure}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{figure}[tb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figs/FigS6-ToggleDetail.pdf}
  \caption{Full budget-toggle detail: paired, onset-aligned counterfactuals at
  $f = 0.20$ over the $19$ included seed pairs per arm, with the dashed line marking the
  toggle at $t_{\mathrm{on}}$ and the pre-toggle traces coinciding by construction. The
  edge-ring traces are averaged over the seed pairs, toggled against control.
  \textbf{(A)}~Largest sell cluster as a fraction of the grid against steps since onset
  under injection (cash $\times 10$ on the tight cash-$500$ base), each toggled
  trajectory drawn over its control.
  \textbf{(B)}~Edge-ring Buy fraction under injection.
  \textbf{(C)}~Edge-ring Sell fraction under injection; the ring flips from the
  control's ${\sim}0.5$ Buy and $0$ Sell to $0$ Buy and ${\sim}1.0$ Sell at the toggle.
  \textbf{(D)}~Largest sell cluster under the mild withdrawal (cash $\times 0.1$ on the
  loose cash-$5{,}000$ base).
  \textbf{(E)}~Edge-ring Buy fraction under the mild withdrawal.
  \textbf{(F)}~Edge-ring Sell fraction under the mild withdrawal.
  \textbf{(G)}~Largest sell cluster under the harsh withdrawal (cash $\times 0.01$).
  \textbf{(H)}~Edge-ring Buy fraction under the harsh withdrawal.
  \textbf{(I)}~Edge-ring Sell fraction under the harsh withdrawal. Either cut lifts the
  ring's Buy fraction from the control's ${\sim}0.46$ after onset to ${\sim}0.72$--$0.74$
  before the transient excess dissipates by ${\sim}200$ steps. The Sell fraction,
  which in this loose configuration declines from ${\sim}0.37$ before onset to zero
  just after, remains indistinguishable between control and toggled throughout.}
  \label{fig:s6}
\end{figure}
